{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n '\"'^#|''^'\"\\\\|' docs/03-plan-typescript-release.md\"","aggregated_output":"1:# Phase 2 plan: Axylo 1.0 in TypeScript\n9:## 1. Public API\n13:### 1.1 Shape\n31:| Decision | Reason |\n32:|---|---|\n33:| Field names over a table | Finding D2: the ten libraries take five incompatible shapes, and the harness had to reshape its fixture a different way for each. Application data arrives as rows. |\n34:| One function per chart type (`line`, `area`, `bar`, `donut`, `scatter`) | Each has its own options type, so options that do not apply are type errors (DX3), and bundlers can drop unused chart types. Whether shared code actually tree-shakes is measured on real consumer builds in M7. |\n35:| Plain options object, not a builder or a JSX tree | The same object works in the React adapter. Recharts needed eight components for the trend task; the information content was four fields. |\n36:| No required configuration beyond data and fields | Findings R11, R15, D1: tooltips, legends, time adapters, margins, colors and accessibility were opt-in somewhere in every library. |\n38:### 1.2 Data: one logical table, three forms\n55:| Rule | Detail |\n56:|---|---|\n57:| Ownership | Row arrays are read once and never mutated. Typed columns are borrowed, not copied; the caller must not change them in place, and replaces them through `update`. |\n58:| Column lengths | All columns must have the same length; otherwise AX100. |\n59:| Missing values | Rows: `null`, or an absent key. Typed columns: `NaN` means missing and does not raise AX103. |\n60:| Order | Line, area and scatter are sorted by x internally (F11). `append` expects rows at or after the last x. |\n61:| Provenance | Every normalized sample keeps the index of the row it came from, so diagnostics, the table and events point at the caller's own data. |\n63:### 1.3 Fields\n78:| Option | Meaning |\n79:|---|---|\n80:| `field` | Key of the row. |\n81:| `label`, `unit` | Axis title; name and unit in tooltip, table and announcements. |\n82:| `type` (x) | `'date'`, `'time'`, `'number'`, `'category'`. Inferred when omitted, except that a number is never inferred to be an instant (F4). |\n83:| `timeZone` (x) | For instants: `'local'` (default), `'UTC'`, or an IANA zone. |\n84:| `format`, `tickFormat` | Readouts and ticks are formatted separately (F6). |\n85:| `scale` (value) | `'percent'` (0–100) or `'ratio'` (0–1). Declared, never inferred (F7). |\n86:| `missing` (value) | `'gap'` (default) or `'zero'` (F2). |\n87:| `parse` (value) | `'number'`, for a text field holding numbers (AX104, AX106). |\n88:| `domain` | Author bounds. Widened to zero on bar and area (F1). |\n90:### 1.4 Common options\n94:| Option | Purpose |\n95:|---|---|\n96:| `title`, `labelledBy`, `subtitle`, `description` | The chart's visible heading and accessible name (A1). `title` may carry a heading `level` and `visible: false`; `labelledBy` points at a heading the page already has. |\n97:| `state` | `'ready'`, `'loading'`, `{ error }`, `{ empty }` (L7). |\n98:| `legend`, `tooltip`, `table` | On by default; each can be turned off. |\n99:| `zoom`, `reference` | Cartesian charts only. |\n100:| `compact` | Line, area and bar: marks only, as a static image with a text alternative (spec 3.7). |\n101:| `colors` | A fixed color per series name (DX6). |\n102:| `messages`, `locale` | Built-in text and formatting locale (A10, F6). |\n103:| `strict`, `onDiagnostic` | Diagnostics delivery (section 3.5 of the spec). |\n107:### 1.5 Handle and commit contract\n120:| Rule | Detail |\n121:|---|---|\n122:| Order | Calls are applied in the order made. `update({ data })` followed by `append` appends to the new data. |\n123:| Two phases | Each call validates and normalizes synchronously: diagnostics are current when it returns, and a `strict` chart throws from that call. Drawing is deferred to the next animation frame, and calls made before that frame are drawn once. |\n124:| Atomic switch | Marks, labels, hit testing and the accessibility layer move to a new data revision together. No frame mixes two revisions. |\n125:| Patches | A key present in an `update` replaces the old value whole; a key set to `undefined` returns to its default; absent keys stay. No deep merging. |\n126:| Rejected changes | A call that throws under `strict` leaves the chart on its last valid state. |\n127:| Streaming | `keep` is the number of distinct x values retained. Rows before the last x are accepted through the slower full update and raise AX151. While zoomed to the latest edge the view follows new data; zoomed elsewhere, it stays put. Paused charts buffer (I6). Keyboard focus stays on its datum until that datum leaves the window (A7). |\n128:| Teardown | `destroy()` cancels the pending frame, disconnects observers and listeners, and makes later calls no-ops. |\n130:### 1.6 Diagnostics\n149:### 1.7 React adapter\n157:| Behavior | Detail |\n158:|---|---|\n159:| Lifecycle | Create on mount, `destroy` on unmount; correct under StrictMode's setup, cleanup, setup. The file is marked `'use client'`. |\n160:| Props to options | Field specs are compared structurally, so an inline `x={{ field: 'ts' }}` does not renormalize on every render. `data` is compared by identity; in development, the same content arriving three times with a new identity raises a hint to memoize it. |\n161:| Removed props | A prop that disappears is sent as `undefined`, which restores the default. |\n162:| Handlers | Replaced without recreating the chart. |\n163:| Streaming | A ref exposes the handle, so `append`, `zoom` and `diagnostics` are reachable. When `data` is controlled, a new `data` prop replaces whatever was appended. |\n167:## 2. Architecture\n169:### 2.1 Pipeline\n181:| Stage | Output | Notes |\n182:|---|---|---|\n183:| normalize | Frame, diagnostics | The only place raw input is read. Never coerces silently. |\n184:| view model | Scales, visible index ranges, series visibility | Domain rules implement F1 and F7. |\n185:| lookup | Nearest-x by binary search; nearest-point by a grid index (scatter) | Works on the frame within the visible range; cost bounded independently of the drawing. |\n186:| layout | Plot rectangle, tick sets, label boxes, legend and control placement | Implements L1, L3, L8. Takes a text-measurement interface as input. |\n187:| scene | Flat list of primitives (`path`, `rect`, `arc`, `rule`, `text`) | Plain data; testable in Node with injected text metrics. |\n188:| renderers | Marks; DOM text; accessibility DOM | See 2.3. |\n190:### 2.2 Invalidation\n194:| Stage | Reruns when |\n195:|---|---|\n196:| normalize | Data revision or field encoding changes. `append` extends the frame without renormalizing earlier rows. |\n197:| view model | Frame revision, zoom, series visibility, author domains. |\n198:| layout | View model; container size; resolved font and text spacing; `document.fonts` finishing a load; locale; any label text. A data-only update reruns layout when the tick labels would change, and skips it otherwise. |\n199:| scene | Layout or view model. |\n200:| mark raster | Scene; device pixel ratio; resolved colors (L4's triggers). |\n204:### 2.3 Rendering\n216:### 2.4 Document structure\n239:### 2.5 Repository\n255:## 3. Milestones\n259:### M0. Foundations (ceiling: 5 days)\n268:### M1. A vertical slice, and the two open decisions (ceiling: 12 days)\n284:### M2. Faithful data, on the line chart (ceiling: 8 days)\n296:### M3. Reading the chart (ceiling: 10 days)\n309:### M4. Accessibility, complete on the line chart (ceiling: 10 days)\n322:### M5. Bar, area, donut, scatter and the compact variant (ceiling: 18 days)\n336:### M6. Updates and scale (ceiling: 10 days)\n348:### M7. Distribution and documentation (ceiling: 8 days)\n359:### M8. Release (ceiling: 10 days)\n370:## 4. Test strategy\n372:| Layer | Tool | What it proves |\n373:|---|---|---|\n374:| Unit | Vitest | Scales, ticks, formatting, parsing, simplification. |\n375:| Property | fast-check | Invariants that must hold for any data: F1, F2, F5, F7, F11, L1. |\n376:| Scene snapshot | Vitest, injected text metrics | Geometry and text for each fixture and width, without a browser. |\n377:| Browser | Playwright on three engines | Real text boxes, resize, pointer, touch, keyboard, time zones, color schemes, reduced motion, fonts loading late. |\n378:| Accessibility | axe-core, accessibility-tree snapshots, keyboard scripts, recorded rehearsal | A1–A10. |\n379:| Visual | Screenshots per operating system | What the scene cannot see: antialiasing, state leaks. |\n380:| Types | `docs/api/examples.ts` pattern | The listed mistakes fail to compile (DX3). |\n381:| Packed distribution | Clean install fixtures | What users actually get (DX1, DX7, DX8, DX10). |\n382:| Acceptance matrix | Data file plus a checker | Every criterion has a test that exists and a current result; stale criterion IDs fail CI. |\n386:## 5. Risks\n388:| Risk | Mitigation |\n389:|---|---|\n390:| The interaction pattern that works in one screen reader fails in another. | Three candidates rehearsed on five pairs in M1, before any of the surrounding DOM is built. Plot roles are internal and can change in a minor release. |\n391:| DOM text costs too much with many charts on a page. | P5 is measured in M1. A fallback to canvas-drawn tick labels would give up user text spacing and forced-colors adaptation for those labels, so it must pass the same L1 and A6 tests before it is allowed. |\n392:| Size targets and features conflict. | Measured from M1; each milestone records its byte cost. Accessibility is not what gets cut; chart types are. |\n393:| The million-point budget cannot be met with exact lookup and gap-preserving simplification. | The headline claim is already limited to measured workloads (spec, section 1). If P2 fails after tuning, the budget is changed in the spec with the measurements, not hidden. |\n394:| Layout promises collide in small tiles. | L8 defines the fallback; the property test covers down to 240 px. |\n395:| One person cannot hand-check real Safari, iOS, Android and Windows screen readers at every milestone. | Automated checks at every milestone; the manual rehearsal at M1, M4 and M8 only, recorded with dates and versions. Device access is a named dependency of those three milestones. |\n396:| Scope growth through chart-type requests. | Section 6. |\n398:## 6. What waits until after 1.0\n400:| Item | Why it can wait |\n401:|---|---|\n402:| More chart types (histogram, heatmap, box plot, candlestick, radar, treemap, sankey, gauge, maps) | Each must pass the full acceptance matrix. Histogram is first in line. |\n403:| Log and other non-linear scales | Need their own rules for zero and negative values. |\n404:| Second y axis | Often misleading; needs a design that keeps F1. |\n405:| Annotations beyond reference lines and bands | Text placement interacts with L1. |\n406:| Linked charts (shared cursor, brush) | Needs a small coordination API. |\n407:| Export (PNG, SVG, CSV), print styling | CSV from the table is the first step. |\n408:| Server-side rendering to SVG | The scene makes it possible. |\n409:| Right-to-left layout mirroring | Stated as a limit in 1.0. |\n410:| Calendar periods beyond year and month (quarters, ISO weeks) | Treated as categories in 1.0. |\n411:| Nested field paths | Flatten rows before passing them. |\n412:| Vue, Svelte, Angular adapters and a custom element | Thin wrappers over the same handle. |\n413:| Public plugin or custom-mark API | After two real uses show its shape. |\n414:| Translations of built-in messages | `messages` makes them possible in 1.0; only English ships. |\n415:| Workers, WebGL or WebGPU, Rust | Decided from measurements (04-plan-performance.md). |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
